      "content": "I'll read both the original test and the CUT source to understand the code before improving it.Now I understand the test well. Key improvements I'll make:\n1. Remove unused imports (`Properties`, `assertFalse`, `assertNotNull`, `assertThrows`, `assertTrue` — then re-add `assertTrue` since I'll use it)\n2. Replace the cryptic `if (!x.equals(...)) assertEquals(...)` pattern with a clear `assertTrue(a || b, message)` that explicitly expresses \"either ordering is acceptable\"\n3. Extract the `toString()` call to a named variable so the intent is obvious\n4. Add a brief comment explaining why both orderings are validThe improved test compiled successfully. The refactored version replaces the cryptic `if (!x.equals(...)) assertEquals(...)` pattern with an explicit `assertTrue(a || b, message)` that clearly communicates \"either ordering is acceptable,\" extracts `toString()` calls into named variables for readability, renames groups to descriptive names (`longOptGroup`, `shortOptGroup`), and removes unused imports."
